<Base title="Brass vs plastic pressure washer fittings" description="Use brass or stainless on pump and hose joints. Plastic collars are acceptable on 1/4 inch consumer tips, not on a 4000 PSI hose nut." path="/brass-vs-plastic/">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Brass vs plastic</p>
    <h1>Brass vs plastic fittings</h1>
    <Answer>
      Anything that sees full pump pressure at a hose or gun joint should be metal. Plastic is for 1/4″ tip sockets you can replace for a few dollars.
    </Answer>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Joint</th><th>Prefer</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Pump outlet</td><td>Brass / steel</td><td>Vibration + full PSI</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hose nuts</td><td>Brass</td><td>Plastic hex rounds off</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gun inlet</td><td>Brass</td><td>You will wrench this once too often</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1/4″ tip socket</td><td>Brass if you can; plastic is common</td><td>Fail-safe is a cheap socket, not a ruined gun</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kärcher K clips</td><td>OEM or metal aftermarket</td><td>UV-aged plastic clips crack</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>PSI printed on a plastic M22 nut is marketing. If the nut is the only thing between you and 3000 PSI, spend the extra $8.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
